\chapter{Source files, tokens, and comments}
\label{ch:6}
\index{Source files, tokens, and comments}

Identifiers use ASCII letters, underscores, and digits after the first character. A hash begins a line comment except when the scanner recognizes the character-literal opener. Whitespace separates tokens. Source locations count byte columns, so a multi-byte UTF-8 value can make a token's byte column differ from its visual position. Preserve original bytes when reporting a diagnostic.

\section{A scanner boundary}
A scanner consumes a pointer and an explicit source length. The C++ entry point rejects embedded NUL and oversized source before the scanner runs. In the generated path, temporary token memory lives in an arena. The callback copies the token into C++ storage before the arena is destroyed. The fallback scanner supplies the same boundary and is tested against the generated scanner for token spelling and positions.

Some syntax distinctions depend on adjacency. For example, an adjacent qualified name such as \OnxCodeTerm{pkg.call} is distinct from spaced dotted syntax used in structural macros. When an import or a macro parses unexpectedly, print the tokens and check the actual characters and offsets. Changing precedence rules to repair a scanner issue risks creating inconsistent generated and fallback behavior.

\section{Reconstruct a diagnostic from bytes}
Imagine a line beginning with a double quote, a lowercase e with an acute
accent encoded as a single Unicode scalar in UTF-8, the closing quote, a
space, and a semicolon. Number the bytes starting at zero. The opening
quote is at zero, the accented letter occupies bytes one and two, the
closing quote is at three, and the space is at four. Thus the semicolon
is at offset five and column six on the first line. Counting visible
characters instead would put it at column five. A diagnostic renderer may
need a mapping to display cells, but changing the scanner's offset to a
display coordinate would make it point at the wrong source byte.

The scanner parity test lets you check this arithmetic independently of a
parser. Its probes report token bytes as hex, so a quote or newline inside
the input need not be inferred from how a terminal displays the token.
For each reported spelling, take that many bytes at its offset in the
original input. They must be exactly the bytes the probe reports. Gaps are
allowed: layout and comments need not appear as tokens. The final entry
has a different rule: its position equals the input length, even when the
last line consists solely of a comment with no terminating newline.

Try inserting a literal zero byte inside that trailing comment. Skipping
ordinary comment text cannot make this file valid: the source-length scan
must reject it at the zero's position before returning any token sequence.

